<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mampuru II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1883</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mampuru II was a claimant to the throne of the Pedi kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the rival of his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekhukhune-i">Sekhukhune I</a>. In 1882 he had Sekhukhune killed and took refuge with the Ndzundza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyabela">Nyabela</a>, whom the South African Republic then besieged. Captured in 1883, he was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, where the central prison now bears his name.</p></div>
